1.1.text:
Article 3
(1) The objectives of providing protection for children and women victims of violence are to:
a. prevent violence against children and women;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against children and women;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for children and women;
d. provide services to children and women victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
(2) The objectives of providing protection for children and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include the aspects of:
a. prevention;
h. services and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

Article 13
(1) The Regional Government implements efforts to protect children and women victims of violence through:
a. implementing policies for the protection of children and women victims of violence as applied by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of children and women victims of violence;
c. formation of PPT;
d. ensuring the implementation of service facilitation to victims;
e. pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and
f. pursuing the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of children and women in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as the basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
(4) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 23
(1) Forms of services provided to children and women victims of violence, are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and 14
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government shall cooperate with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, community, family and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services, and handling of children and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF KONAWE,

Considering : a. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence;
b. that children and women are vulnerable groups experiencing violence where all forms of violence against children and women constitute violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity thus requiring legal protection to protect their dignity and honor and to guarantee their right to life in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination;
c. that violence against children and women in Konawe Regency shows quite high numbers and continues to increase, thus requiring protection efforts;
d. that all forms of violence against children and women constitute violations of human rights thus their dignity and honor need to be protected and their right to life guaranteed in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination;
e. that the implementation of Protection of Children and Women Victims of Violence in Konawe Regency needs to be supported by institutions and regulations so as to guarantee its implementation;
f. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, letter c, letter d and letter e above, it is deemed necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Protection of Children and Women Victims of Violence in Konawe Regency;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 29 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions in Sulawesi (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 74, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1822);
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on the Elimination of all Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning 2 Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
9. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as last amended by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
11. Law 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
12. Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
13. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Implementation of Women's Protection;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Implementation of Child Protection;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards (SPM) and Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;

With Mutual Approval
THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF KONAWE REGENCY
